        "$numberInt": "31"
      },
      "name": "Childhood Nikah",
      "slug": "childhood-nikah",
      "description": "<p class=\"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal\">Childhood nikah based novels follow a theme close to many South Asian families — a marriage arranged between two children, often cousins, long before either understands what it means. Years later, the story picks up when both are adults, sometimes unaware of the nikah until circumstances force it into the open, or fully aware but distant from each other emotionally.</p><p class=\"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal\"><strong>Ishq Ki Dastan by Alishba Hassan</strong> captures this theme well, following a couple whose bond was decided in childhood but tested as adults. <strong>Dil Tery Bin Dhadke Na by Aneeqa Ch</strong> also explores a similar setup, where past decisions shape present-day relationships. <strong>Kanwal Kay Phool by Husnay Kanwal</strong> adds a cousin-marriage angle common in this genre, where family ties and romance intertwine.</p><p>\r\n\r\n</p><p class=\"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal\">If you enjoy Urdu novels where the marriage came first and the relationship had to be built years later, this collection is written around that exact idea. All titles are free to read online or download as PDF.</p>",
