      "_id": {
        "$numberInt": "34"
      },
      "name": "Contract Marriage",
      "slug": "contract-marriage",
      "description": "<p class=\"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal\">Contract marriage novels follow a setup where two people agree to marry for practical reasons — financial need, family pressure, or a business-like arrangement — rather than love. What makes this genre engaging is watching that purely transactional bond slowly turn into genuine affection as the characters spend more time living as a married couple.</p><p class=\"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal\"><strong>Jo Chaho Tum by Ali Shah</strong> is a well-known example, following a couple who start off with clear terms and boundaries that gradually blur as feelings develop. <strong>Wafa Ki Talash by Nasif Awan</strong> explores a similar dynamic, where trust has to be rebuilt from a purely contractual beginning. <strong>Pehli Nazar Ki Mohabbat by Syeda Hemail Fatima</strong> also fits this theme, following characters navigating an arrangement that wasn't supposed to involve emotions at all.</p><p>\r\n\r\n</p><p class=\"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal\">If you enjoy Urdu novels where love isn't the starting point but something that develops despite the odds, this collection is built around exactly that. All titles are free to read online or download as PDF.</p>",
